{"title":"Vivid Career Courses","sub":"Sell to learners or sell to employers?","type":"Growth strategy","industry":"Online education","geo":"United Kingdom","diff":2,"fmt":"Interviewer-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"B2C vs B2B · customer lifetime value · renewals","prompt":"Vivid sells online courses in data and digital skills to individual learners for $400 each, mostly through online ads. Growth has stalled as ad costs rise. Its head of sales wants to start selling seats to employers for staff training. The CEO asks whether the employer route is worth building.","clarify":[{"q":"Individual learners?","a":"Each costs about $220 in ads to win. Delivery (platform, tutors, certificates) costs $40 a learner. About 10% buy a second course later, at a $30 marketing cost."},{"q":"Employers?","a":"Vivid would charge $250 a seat. A typical deal is 200 seats and costs about $15,000 of sales effort."},{"q":"Renewals?","a":"Similar providers see about 70% of employers renew each year, at a small account-management cost of about $10 a seat."}],"structure":{"root":"Should Vivid build an employer (B2B) business?","branches":[{"head":"Profit per seat, year 1","items":["Price","Cost to win","Delivery"]},{"head":"Profit over time","items":["Renewals vs repeat purchases"]},{"head":"What it takes","items":["Sales team","Content for employers","Reporting"]}]},"hypothesis":"Per seat, employer deals look worse because the price is lower. But employers renew, so the cost to win is paid once and the profit repeats. I'd compare over three years.","questions":[{"q":"Compare first-year profit per learner or seat.","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p>Individual: $400 − $220 − $40 = <b>$140</b>. Employer seat: $250 − $15,000 ÷ 200 − $40 = <b>$135</b>.</p> <p>About the same in year 1, despite the lower price, because winning a seat costs far less than winning an individual.</p>"},{"q":"Now compare over three years.","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" wi